Invalid example packages that tack test rejects, one case per package, in
byte order of name.

A case named n is its files, each a txtar file n/<path>, followed by
n/expected, what tack test reports for it, with each file's path printed
as n/<path>. The case is built as tack test builds a package: from the
directory n, or the one an optional n/.target file names, relative to n,
its test files, those whose names end in _test.tck, read with its other
files, and the main tack test generates for them added; what is reported
in that main is dropped when anything else is an error. A directory is
built in its module, so every case has an n/tack.mod. Every file must end
with a newline. internal/invalid documents the format; go test ./test
-run TestInvalidTests checks every case, and -update rewrites the
expected sections.

-- errorf-format/tack.mod --
module errorf
-- errorf-format/errorf_test.tck --
package errorf

import testing from "std/testing"

func test_format(mut t testing.T) error {
    mut t.errorf("%d is not %s", "seven", 7)
    return noerror
}
-- errorf-format/expected --
stage: typeck
---

